::CW_U03_PRE_Q01::[MLA.CW.FIC.02] Passage: A character gives up a winning speech slot to help a nervous friend prepare. What universal theme is best supported?{
~A. Speeches are always short.#Incorrect. Length is not the focus. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Friendship prevents all ambition.#Incorrect. The theme is balance, not removing ambition.
~C. Winning never matters.#Incorrect. The slot matters because giving it up is a sacrifice.
=D. Personal goals can still leave room for helping others.#Correct. The character sacrifices attention to support a friend.
}

::CW_U03_PRE_Q02::[MLA.CW.FIC.04] Source element: A guide gives an object before a journey. Adaptation: A coach gives a route card before a city race. What is preserved?{
~A. The exact original setting.#Incorrect. The setting changes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A guide supports a journey with an object.#Correct. The coach and route card preserve the guide-object pattern.
~C. A copied plot.#Incorrect. The scene is transformed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. No source connection.#Incorrect. The source pattern remains. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_PRE_Q03::[MLA.CW.FIC.06] Central idea: Avoiding responsibility can make guilt grow. Which detail best supports it?{
~A. The wall is painted white.#Incorrect. This is unrelated by itself. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A dog walks outside.#Incorrect. This does not connect to guilt. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The character hides the broken key after blaming someone else.#Correct. Hiding evidence after blame shows guilt. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The character eats breakfast.#Incorrect. This does not support responsibility. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_PRE_Q04::[MLA.CW.FIC.07] Passage: "Naturally, the committee solved the leak by buying a larger bucket." What rhetorical choice is strongest?{
=A. Sarcasm criticizing an ineffective solution.#Correct. The sentence mocks the committee's poor response.
~B. Neutral admiration.#Incorrect. The tone is critical. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A scientific report.#Incorrect. The passage is rhetorical. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A theme comparison.#Incorrect. The question asks rhetoric. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_PRE_Q05::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Passage: Eli thinks family stories delay chores until he finds the same street names in his grandmother's old notebook. What changes?{
~A. Eli proves chores are impossible.#Incorrect. Chores are not the main realization. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Eli recognizes that the stories carry family history.#Correct. The notebook changes his perspective. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Eli stops listening forever.#Incorrect. The evidence suggests he values the stories.
~D. The notebook has no role.#Incorrect. It provides evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_PRE_Q06::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage A: Lina practices alone after failure. Passage B: Theo asks for help after failure. What comparison is strongest?{
~A. The passages are identical because both have names.#Incorrect. Names are surface details. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Only Passage A has a theme.#Incorrect. Both have themes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Failure ends growth.#Incorrect. Both continue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Both show growth after failure, but one emphasizes independent persistence while the other emphasizes support.#Correct. This compares shared theme and different development.
}

::CW_U03_PRE_Q07::[MLA.CW.WR.04] Passage: Talia thinks asking for critique proves weakness, but later asks what to revise first. What coming-of-age change is shown?{
=A. She learns feedback can be part of growth.#Correct. Her belief changes. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. She refuses all revision.#Incorrect. She asks what to revise. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. She stops making art.#Incorrect. No evidence says that. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. She avoids the teacher.#Incorrect. She asks the teacher. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_PRE_Q08::[MLA.CW.FIC.02] What is a complete theme statement?{
